  "gotchas": [
    "Auth: one token server for both environments — the docs' Multi-Environment Integrations example sends either environment's credentials to https://idp.flutterwave.com/realms/flutterwave/protocol/openid-connect/token, and Authentication says \"This process applies to both the sandbox and production environments. Make sure to use the correct credentials for each environment to obtain a valid token.\" \"Each token is valid for **10 minutes**\"; \"We recommend refreshing the token at least one minute before it expires.\" Cache the token. Sandbox: \"We archive your test data after 30 days.\"",
    "Headers: send X-Idempotency-Key on this POST. Docs (Idempotency): \"For all `POST` endpoints, include an idempotency key in the request header (`X-Idempotency-Key`).\" The API reference marks it optional; the headers guide lists it under 'Mandatory API Headers'. A reused key replays the first response (\"the response will include the header `X-Idempotency-Cache-Hit: true`\"), so reuse the key when retrying the same operation and use a new one for a new operation. X-Trace-Id is optional. For both: \"It must be a string using only alphanumeric ASCII characters.\" \"Its length should be between 12 and 255 characters.\" The idempotency page also says \"We recommend using a **UUID** string as your idempotency key\": strip the UUID's hyphens to satisfy both.",
    "Docs (General Transfer Flow): \"The transfer sender entity is only required for transfers in `EGP`, `INR`, `EUR`, and `GBP`.\" Pass data.id (sdr_…) as payment_instruction.sender_id.",
    "type: bank_egp and mobile_money_egp need name, phone, email, address, date_of_birth and national_identification (with expiration_date); bank_gbp and bank_eur need name, phone, email, address; generic_sender needs name. Open question: no sender type is named for INR (generic_sender is the likely one).",
    "phone.number is 7-10 digits without the country code; country_code 1-3 digits.",
    "Errors: {\"status\":\"failed\",\"error\":{\"type\",\"code\",\"message\",\"validation_errors\"}}; error.code is a string and 400s carry validation_errors[{field_name,message}]. Besides the generic codes (10400 REQUEST_NOT_VALID, 10401, 10403 FORBIDDEN, 10404 RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND, 10409 RESOURCE_CONFLICT, 10422 UNPROCESSABLE, 10500) the error table documents these for this endpoint: 280409 SENDER_ALREADY_EXISTS, 281400 UNKNOWN_SENDER_TYPE. Branch on error.type / error.code, not on the HTTP status alone. Docs (Errors): \"The current limit is 500 API requests per minute.\" A 429 can come back as a non-JSON page (\"HTML error pages such as `Cloudflare 1015 responses`\"): parse error bodies defensively and back off exponentially with jitter."
  ],
